WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.877
This guided practice builds a department-level report.

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:08.287
The requirement says every department must appear, including departments with zero activity.

00:00:08.287 --> 00:00:13.832
That tells us to start from departments and preserve that table.

00:00:13.832 --> 00:00:17.582
Admissions and medical staff are optional related data, so we LEFT JOIN them.

00:00:17.582 --> 00:00:22.032
A department can have several admissions and several staff members.

00:00:22.032 --> 00:00:27.412
Because both are one-to-many relationships, the joined detail rows can multiply.

00:00:27.412 --> 00:00:33.892
That is why the query uses COUNT DISTINCT for admission IDs, patient IDs, and staff IDs.

00:00:33.892 --> 00:00:40.852
The query also calculates average stay length and combines staff specializations into a readable list.

00:00:40.852 --> 00:00:45.082
After grouping, the final grain is one row per department.

00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:48.377
Run the query and find the department with no activity.

00:00:48.377 --> 00:00:52.782
It should still appear because departments is the preserved population.

00:00:52.782 --> 00:00:57.712
Then inspect a busy department and compare its counts with the underlying data.

00:00:57.712 --> 00:01:04.892
This structure is common in management dashboards where every region, program, department,

00:01:04.892 --> 00:01:07.992
or product category must remain visible even when activity is zero.
